<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tacna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tacna is a city in the far south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, near the Chilean border. Occupied by Chile during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> in 1880, it remained under Chilean administration for nearly fifty years because the plebiscite promised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ancon">Treaty of Ancón</a> was never held. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> of 1929 returned Tacna to Peru, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> remained Chilean.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tacna.jpg" alt="Tacna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marco Antonio Correa Flores · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catedral_Nuestra_Se%C3%B1ora_del_Rosario_de_Tacna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the Pacific reshaped the map of South America, leaving Bolivia without a coastline, a loss Bolivia has never accepted, and giving Chile the provinces of Tarapacá and Antofagasta, while the dispute over <b>Tacna</b> and Arica was settled with Peru only by the Treaty of Lima of 1929.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Chile took Tarapacá, <b>Tacna</b> and Arica and occupied Lima in January 1881, while Andrés Avelino Cáceres led guerrilla resistance in the central highlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Military</a></li></ul></section>
